Z||000012||Fiero-lite||05-11-2000||06:59 PM||||Interesting discussion.  Hope nobody minds if I post my two cents.<BR>The "archaic OHV 5rpm engine" has the ability for VTEC like technology already designed in.  Variable Valve Timing allows for proper breathing of the engine at different RPM speeds.  This can be achieved by letting the intake & exhaust valves "overlap" in timing when the engine is at high rev's and not when it is turning slowly. Of course, racing cams allow overlap, but at all engine speeds, causing that lopy idle (not to mention failed smog tests).  VTEC style engines hydraulically change the intake, exhaust or both cam timing or profiles to allow different breathing at different engine speeds.  They take advantage of the fact that the intake and exhaust valves are governed by separate cams.  Many pushrod engines have hydraulic actuators that govern the valve timing independently for each valve.  Lifters!  Hydraulic lifters can be designed to allow valve overlap only at high revs, but it’s not a simple matter of  throwing in a set of different lifters and Viola, more power.  Leak-down or Rhodes lifters can be used to provide a designed “pump-down” time causing the valve to begin closing independently of cam profile.  <BR>Very basically,  it works like this:  At low engine speeds, lifter leak-down time is a certain small fraction of the time it takes the engine to make one revolution.  At high engine speeds, this same lifter leak-down time is a much larger fraction of the time it takes the engine to make one revolution since the high speed “one rev-time” is shorter but the leak-down time remains the same.  This means that the valve stays open longer (as measured in degrees, not time) during high revs only.  Lifter and cam design are done together to achieve the desired result.  Not a simple matter.  Why aren’t these lifters always used?  Good question.  Engine longevity is a function of engine rev’s- lower engine speeds theoretically allow for longer lasting engines with cheaper parts.  Mabey this is part of the “Big Three” attitude of design.  One thing that sells cars these days is a long, comprehensive warranty.  But I digress.  Search on “Rhodes Lifters” and see what you come up with. I’d be interested in learning more myself.  I think good things await Fiero owners if the  design issues can be worked out.  If the correct lifters and cam are found/designed, the parts swap would be much easier than going VTEC on our engines.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||FIEROCIOUS||05-12-2000||12:44 PM||||I do belive they make Rhoads lifter for the duke however, they are flat tappet style and you would lose the roller lifter. Rhoads however does not make lifters for the 2.8 V6<P>As far as Vtec I've done many (many) convertions in Civics matter of factly I'm about to finish one this week, I own a 1.6 DOHC Vtec.<P>The Vtec is not I repeat DOES NOT CHANGE overlap or duration at least not in the way suggested it changes lift via rocker ratio. <P>Vtec does stand for variable valve timing but in reality it is variable (set) lift.<P>The Vtec sylinoid is activated at 3500 RPM this allows oil pressure to activate a set of rockers on the exhaust side to creat more lift NOT DURATION. Toyota has a system that rotates the cam (this changes overlap) on the market now.<P>Lets fantisize a little more....<BR>Valves controlled by sylnoids, any lift at any RPM and any duration at any RPM all controlled by computer. you want lopy ide clickity tap on the computer (laptop) and wola your car shakes rattles and doesn't idle for crap! <P>But again your friends will think your cool and all the chicks will dig you! And all said and done you will have to put " The Magnifisent " after your name! LOL<BR>J/K ||||reg||1
